estamp \"\\\\n\" response \"\\\\n\\\\n\")\n        (let ((coding-system-for-write 'utf-8))\n          (append-to-file (point-min) (point-max) log-path))))))'''\n\nnew = '''(defun iar--cycle-log-append (agent-name start end)\n  \"Append the latest LLM turn to audit/<agent-name>/cycle.log.\nSTART and END are buffer positions delimiting the new response\nregion.  Creates the log file if it does not exist.  Prepends a\ntimestamp.\n\nc237 (2026-09-22): the old docstring said \"Log ONLY the new response\nregion\" while the code appended whatever region the post-response\nhook delivered -- and before the fork's :turn-start marker (gptel\nc237), that region was the ENTIRE conversation on every tool-loop\nrun (84% of both cycle logs was duplication; the c233 fragment was\nextracted from a delimiter pair inside a tool-result preview in\nexactly this dump shape).  The comment described a per-turn model\nthat never existed.  Two fixes, defense in depth:\n\n1. The fork now fires post-response hooks with per-turn regions\n   (:turn-start marker, gptel-request.el gptel--handle-wait).\n2. This function walks the region by `gptel' text property and\n   excludes `ignore' spans (reasoning/thinking) and (tool . ID)\n   spans (tool-call display + tool-result previews) -- the c132\n   discipline the sentinel check and the one-shot extractor already\n   carry.  A delimiter inside a tool preview can no longer land in\n   cycle.log even if a future region regression re-widens it.\n\nThe walk keeps MODEL TEXT and SEPARATORS (nil / `response' spans).\nbuffer-substring-no-properties remains deliberate per span: the log\nis a record surface, not a judgment surface -- but the RECORD now\nmeans the model's words, not the scaffolding around them.\"\n  (when (and (integerp start) (integerp end) (< start end))\n    (let* ((project (iar--current-project-name))\n           (log-path (expand-file-name\n                      (format \"%s/%s/cycle.log\" project agent-name)\n                      (expand-file-name iar-audit-path iar-personalization-path)))\n           (timestamp (format-time-string \"[%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S]\"))\n           ;...[+4199 chars]
